#            4a8d306 — the `paid` state (0107). features.md draws the lifecycle
#              as `completed → (paid) → review`; 0001 shipped the fourteen
#              states either side and left the gate out, so payment lived only
#              in paid_status/paid_at and a valuation waiting on money was
#              indistinguishable, in the state column, from one waiting on an
#              analyst. The migration adds the enum value BEFORE 'review' and
#              nothing else on purpose: Postgres permits ALTER TYPE ... ADD
#              VALUE inside a transaction (the runner wraps every migration in
#              one) but forbids USING it there, so anything referencing 'paid'
#              waits for a later migration.
#
#              The parentheses are load-bearing. Both `completed → paid` and
#              `completed → review` are legal, and nextState() diverts through
#              the gate only when payment actually settled (paid_by_partner
#              counts — settled money the client never saw an invoice for).
#              Stripe is still unconfigured here, so every live valuation is
#              `unpaid`; a gate that defaulted to closed would have stranded
#              all of them at `completed`. That fallback is why nextState takes
#              context instead of AUTO_ADVANCE simply pointing at 'paid'.
#
#              Settlement moves the workflow: the Stripe fulfil path advances a
#              `completed` valuation to `paid` in the same patch that records
#              the money, then fires onStateChanged, so partner webhooks and
#              the notification matrix see it. Only from `completed` — money on
#              a file still in intake, or already in review, says nothing about
#              where the work got to. Deliberately NOT added to
#              TIMESTAMP_ON_STATE: paid_at belongs to the payment layer, and
#              stamping it on entry would overwrite the true settlement time
#              and double-assign the column in one UPDATE.
